INT. VILLAGE HALL - FUNERAL RECEPTION - DAY

Orsolya, 60s, in a black coat stitched with tiny silver thread that moves when nobody is looking, stands by the tea urn while the mourners murmur and the rain taps the leaded windows and a dog under the buffet table sighs, and across the room Bram, 30s, the dead man's nephew, holds a paper plate of untouched sandwiches and watches the coffin lid through the open vestry door as though it might speak.

THE CHOIR gathers by the piano, six old women in grey, humming a note that is slightly too low for the room.

ORSOLYA
You haven't asked me why I came. Everyone else has. They ask it with their eyebrows. Your uncle owed me a debt, Bram, and in my family we collect, even from the dead. Especially from the dead, who are very bad at hiding.

BRAM
(beat)
He owed everyone. He owed the butcher. It's a funeral, not an audit.

ORSOLYA
He owed me his shadow.

The urn hisses. The humming from the choir climbs a half step.

BRAM
That's not funny.

ORSOLYA
It wasn't meant to be. Look at the floor beneath the coffin. Go on. The vicar has shuffled four times and still it hasn't moved with him.

Bram looks. A dark shape lies against the vestry tiles, long and patient, pointing the wrong way from the candles.

BRAM
(quietly)
That's the light from the door.

ORSOLYA
Is it?

THE CHOIR
(in unison, low)
Amen.

A VOICE (O.S.)
Bram? Bram, love, are you in there? Your aunt wants the car brought round.

BRAM
(calling back)
In a minute, Mrs. Pell!

ORSOLYA
Do not answer him when he speaks. That is the only rule I will give you. He will use your voice, because it is the nearest one, and he will use your mother's after that.

BRAM
(beat)
Speak. Who speaks?

The vestry candles bend flat, all together, as if a door had opened onto a great cold field. The choir stops humming. The dog under the table stands up, hackles raised, and does not make a sound.

A VOICE (V.O.)
(in Bram's own tones, warm)
Bram. It's all right. Come and help me with this lid.

ORSOLYA
(very still)
That is the first.

BRAM
(whispering)
How do I stop it?

ORSOLYA
You give it what it is owed. And you do not flinch, boy, whatever it wears.

The shadow on the tiles lifts one edge, slowly, like a hand testing a curtain.
